{"id_": "3016cde9-32b1-454c-8ec1-676f47a32848", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"pubmed_id": "10135926", "question": "Is oral endotracheal intubation efficacy impaired in the helicopter environment?", "final_decision": "yes"}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": [], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": [], "relationships": {}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "
", "text": "Patients transported by helicopter often require advanced airway management. The purpose of this study was to determine whether or not the in-flight environment of air medical transport in a BO-105 helicopter impairs the ability of flight nurses to perform oral endotracheal intubation. The study was conducted in an MBB BO-105 helicopter. Flight nurses performed three manikin intubations in each of the two study environments: on an emergency department stretcher and in-flight in the BO-105 helicopter. The mean time required for in-flight intubation (25.9 +/- 10.9 seconds) was significantly longer than the corresponding time (13.2 +/- 2.8 seconds) required for intubation in the control setting (ANOVA, F = 38.7, p 0.05). INTRODUCTION SETTING METHODS RESULTS Air Ambulances Analysis of Variance Data Collection Emergency Nursing Humans Intubation, Intratracheal North Carolina Time and Motion Studies Transportation of Patients Treatment Outcome y e s y e s", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": null, "end_char_idx": null, "text_template": "{metadata_str}

