{"id_": "23f18fe6-7737-4109-8774-1d2714f68612", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"pubmed_id": "15125825", "question": "Starting insulin in type 2 diabetes: continue oral hypoglycemic agents?", "final_decision": "yes"}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": [], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": [], "relationships": {}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "
", "text": "To evaluate the effects of insulin 30/70 twice daily or bedtime isophane (NPH) insulin plus continued sulfonylurea and metformin in patients with type 2 diabetes in primary care. Open-label, randomized trial. Persons younger than 76 years with type 2 diabetes whose disease had not been controlled with oral hypoglycemic agents alone. A total of 64 insulin-naive patients treated with maximal feasible dosages of sulfonylurea and metformin (baseline glycosylated hemoglobin [HbA1c]=8.5%) were randomly assigned to insulin monotherapy (IM group; n=31) or insulin in addition to unchanged oral hypoglycemic medication (IC group; n=33) for 12 months. Insulin doses were adjusted to obtain fasting glucose<7.0 mmol/L and postprandial glucose<10.0 mmol/L.", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": null, "end_char_idx": null, "text_template": "{metadata_str}

{content}", "class_name": "TextNode"}

{"id_": "a96d3a57-0c6b-44e4-afdf-cc86dec413ac", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"pubmed_id": "15125825", "question": "Starting insulin in type 2 diabetes: continue oral hypoglycemic agents?", "final_decision": "yes"}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": [], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": [], "relationships": {}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "
", "text": "Insulin doses were adjusted to obtain fasting glucose<7.0 mmol/L and postprandial glucose<10.0 mmol/L. Outcome measures included HbA1c, treatment failure, weight, hypoglycemic events and symptoms, satisfaction with treatment, general well-being, and fear of injecting insulin and testing. HbA1c improved from 8.3% to 7.6% in the IC group, and from 8.8% to 7.6% in the IM group (P=NS). The IC group had 24% treatment failures, compared with 2% in the IM group (P=.09). Patients in the IC group had less weight gain than those in the IM group (1.3 vs 4.2 kg; P=.01), and they reported fewer hypoglycemic events (2.7 vs 4.3; P=.02).", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": null, "end_char_idx": null, "text_template": "{metadata_str}

{content}", "class_name": "TextNode"}

{"id_": "6557d289-5bbc-4ed4-bef5-952848f6fbf0", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"pubmed_id": "15125825", "question": "Starting insulin in type 2 diabetes: continue oral hypoglycemic agents?", "final_decision": "yes"}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": [], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": [], "relationships": {}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "
", "text": "Increased satisfaction with treatment was equal in the 2 groups, and general well-being improved by 3.0 points more in the IC group (P=.05). Fear of self-injecting and self-testing did not differ. OBJECTIVE STUDY DESIGN POPULATION OUTCOMES MEASURED RESULTS Analysis of Variance Diabetes Mellitus, Type 2 Drug Therapy, Combination Female Glycated Hemoglobin A Humans Hypoglycemic Agents Insulin Insulin, Isophane Male Metformin Middle Aged Sulfonylurea Compounds y e s y e s", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": null, "end_char_idx": null, "text_template": "{metadata_str}

